const SHU392_BRIEF = "Card SHU-392: the store, the field rules and the self-edit service for a person's names, objective, introduction and preferred time (S2 part two, PD-10 and PD-11 text fields; risk R2). This is the first half of SHU-372's contract; SHU-393 adds the routes and the My profile page on top of it, so this card adds no route, page or gateway file.\n\nYou are working in the StudentHub platform monorepo at the bound head, which includes SHU-371 (written values over the snapshot, the person rule, the language page flow). Dependencies are installed and there is no network. You may run npm run typecheck, npm test and node --test on built files under dist/. The Postgres tests (npm run test:db) cannot run here, so write them carefully; CI runs them on the card's pull request. Earlier cards of this queue may have landed before you: re-read any line this brief names.\n\nHow this brief is written. It states what must be true and what must never happen. It does not prescribe the code: choose the shape, the queries and the helpers yourself, following the patterns of the files it names. When a guard and the code you would naturally write disagree, the guard wins; when the brief and the code at the bound head disagree, say so in your final message rather than forcing either.\n\nWhy. Legacy lets a person change each of these from the app (AccountController::actionUpdateName, actionUpdateNameAr, actionUpdateObjective, actionUpdateIntro, actionUpdatePreferredTime); the platform has no store for any of them. This card adds the store every later self-edit shares and its first five fields. Column names follow the mapping table in the queue file so the import (SHU-103) loads legacy values into the same columns. SHU-372's contract ran six times as one card and never merged: each build wrote about twenty files across the database, the domain and the gateway, and the reviews kept finding new defects. This half is smaller on purpose; build all of it in the first build.\n\nRead first:\n- packages/safe-write-contract (createSafeWrite, FieldPolicy, validateChange) and packages/db/src/safe-write-store.ts (receipts are rows of authorization_mutation_audit under operation profile.safe_write; a receipt carries no value).\n- The current authorization_audit_summary_valid: find the latest migration in packages/db/migrations that defines it (0205_organization_currency.sql at the bound head) and read the whole CASE.\n- Migration 0183 (the forbidden code-point list, how a CHECK mirrors an application rule, and a name CHECK that allows hyphens).\n- packages/profile/src/profile.ts (the field parsers and maximum lengths: your rules accept every value the parser accepts for these fields, except one-word names) and packages/profile/src/fixtures.ts (\"Noor Al-Sabah\").\n- PD-10 in docs/parity/profile-and-private-documents.md (at least two words, nothing more).\n\nOutcomes: acceptance items 1 to 6.\n\nGuards, each from a review of an earlier run of this contract:\n- The audit validator. CREATE OR REPLACE replaces the whole function, so copy the current body exactly and change only the profile.safe_write field list. SHU-390 copied an older body (0147's) and every organization, pay-contract, bank-details and profile-record write failed its audit CHECK. Add a Postgres test that writes an audit row for one operation 0205 accepts and this card does not touch, and that it still succeeds.\n- One rule, two places. The application and the CHECK accept and refuse exactly the same values. SHU-390's objective CHECK admitted \"a  b\", which the application never produces; SHU-388's letters-only words refused \"Noor Al-Sabah\". Put each rule's examples in SHU372_RULES and run the same values through the application test and the Postgres CHECK test.\n- Locale. A POSIX class such as [[:alpha:]] follows the cluster's LC_CTYPE, and under C it matches ASCII only, so an Arabic name the application accepts would fail the CHECK. Make the letter test independent of the cluster's locale, for example by evaluating the regular expression under an explicit collation whose classes are Unicode-defined (PostgreSQL 17's builtin pg_c_utf8 or an ICU collation) after checking that the CI and deploy images have it, or by spelling the same code-point ranges out in both places; say which you chose and why.\n- NFC. Normalise to NFC in the application and check IS NFC NORMALIZED in the CHECK.\n- Store. The commit checks the receipt and change-set digest the way PostgresSafeWriteStore does. readOwnPreferences is one read keyed by the principal (a LEFT JOIN from the person's row, never a FULL JOIN filtered afterwards), and each field carries its own written-at date, never the latest write's.\n- Receipts across cards. Both profile cards write receipts under profile.safe_write for the same principal; this card's receipt read returns null for a receipt of another field rather than throwing, and safe-write-store.ts's language read does the same for one of these five fields, with a test each.\n- Clearing. Add allowClear to FieldPolicy in types.ts; validateChange accepts an empty value only when it is set and counts code points ([...value].length). Add a test in safe-write-contract.test.ts that an opted-in policy accepts \"\" and the default policy refuses it.\n- Mutants. self-edit-mutations.mjs follows packages/db/test/safe-write-mutations.mjs: a token per mutant, applied to the built modules, each run against its own token's test and dying by assertion.\n\nStanding lines, held by every card of this queue: (S1) for every value this card writes or changes, say in your final message where it is read and shown to a person, and a test proves it shows there; (S2) no request makes one query or port call per row, and every new lookup column is indexed.\n\nnpm test and npm run test:db find test files and mutation scripts by name, and the schema_migrations lists are built from packages/db/migrations. Add tests and mutation scripts as files; do not edit package.json or those lists. Name every new test with its token first and a space after it.\n\nBefore you finish:\n- Run git status and make sure every changed or new file is one of your paths. Delete any scratch file, script or output you created elsewhere (gitignored output such as dist/ and node_modules/ is fine).\n- Put each new test in one of the test files your paths name; do not add a test file under another name.\n- If something the brief asks for can only be done by changing a file outside your paths, do not change that file: leave it, finish everything else, and answer BLOCKED with the file's path and why in your summary.\n- Finish with npm run typecheck and npm test passing, run to the end. If a test cannot run in your sandbox, say which one and why rather than working around it. In your final message, list the files you changed, how each acceptance item is pinned, and answer S1.\n\nOut of scope for both halves: choice fields (SHU-373), phone and profile link (SHU-374), completeness, staff editing a person (S6), CSS beyond existing classes, JavaScript in the page, package.json, the lockfile, and any network call, credential, staging or production access.";
